<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#assertion_about_01269> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#kermit_the_frog_character> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Emotion> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Emotion Expression elements from the image. Emotion Expression includes: facial expressions, emotional gestures, mood indicators, affective elements, and emotional cues. For each emotional element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'mischievous_smile', 'concerned_expression'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the mischievous smile', 'the concerned expression'), 3) A brief description of the emotional state or expression. Focus on clearly visible emotional indicators that convey feelings or moods. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"mischievous_smile\", \"label\": \"the mischievous smile\", \"description\": \"A knowing, slightly devious smile suggesting mischief or satisfaction\"}"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#kermit_not_muslim_mapping> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme uses the visual entity 'Kermit_the_Frog' as a stand-in for the textual concept 'definitely not a Muslim'. The text explicitly states that Kermit is 'definitely not a Muslim', creating an analogical mapping where the fictional character is projected onto the religious identity to mock or dismiss Muslims. This mapping is central to the meme's harmful intent, as it leverages Kermit's non-Muslim, fictional nature to stereotype and dehumanize Muslims."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#assertion_about_01269> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#asserts> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#top_text> .
