<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#AnalogicalMapping> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Analogical Mapping is a mapping between one entity and another in the meme, usually between a visual element and a textual element. It follows the pattern: 'X is Y'. E.g. the juxtaposition of a certain text to some visual material creates a mapping between the text and the visual material."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#top_text> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#kermit_the_frog_character> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Kermit the Frog is a fictional character from the Muppet franchise, created by Jim Henson. His widely recognized image is used in the meme to contrast with the text 'definitely not a Muslim'. Understanding that Kermit is a fictional, non-human character is essential to interpreting the meme's satirical or mocking intent."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#consilientWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "amplifies"@en .
