_:neaa3726a7ba34412820b871e80d4a10ab30 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Object-embedded> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text naturally appearing on clothing, objects, and surfaces within meme scenes"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#religious_identity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme is designed to target the Muslim community by using the text 'definitely not a Muslim' in juxtaposition with Kermit the Frog, a non-Muslim fictional character, to mock or dismiss Muslims. The intent is to ridicule or belittle this religious group by associating them with a cartoon character, which is a form of cultural or religious mockery."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the textual material 'definitely not a Muslim' which is overlaid on the image of Kermit the Frog. The meme uses this text to imply that the user (or the viewer) is projecting their own understanding or judgment onto the character, interpreting Kermit as a non-Muslim to mock or dismiss Muslims. There are no explicit deictic pronouns like 'me' or 'you', but the projection is implicit through the viewer's engagement with the text and its association with the visual material. The user is not directly projected onto a visual material or scene, but rather onto the textual material which serves as the primary vehicle for the harmful stereotype."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VerbalIrony> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Contradiction> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#temporallyRelatedTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates one dimension being temporally related with another in memes, e.g. text adding before/after context to visual material"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualWorldPart> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#narrativeFlowAdjacentTo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#descriptivelyAlignedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#verballyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#ContextualReferentAdjacency> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#EntityOcclusion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text strategically hiding or revealing specific objects or entities in memes"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#PerimeterFraming> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text surrounding visual content as a frame or border in memes"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#Kermit_the_Frog> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A green, anthropomorphic frog puppet with large eyes, a wide smile, and a distinctive collar. He is seated on a white block, waving with one hand. This character is widely recognized from the Muppet franchise and is the central visual element of the meme."@en .
_:neaa3726a7ba34412820b871e80d4a10ab72 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Toxicity> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#muslim_stereotype> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme relies on the implicit association of 'definitely not a Muslim' with Kermit the Frog to mock or dismiss Muslims. This leverages a harmful stereotype that reduces a religious group to a caricature by juxtaposing them with a non-Muslim character. The stereotype is not visible in the image but is essential to understanding the meme's intent and toxicity."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Contradiction> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#colorMatchedIntegratedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates text adopting and harmonizing with the visual color schemes and palette in memes"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01269#religious_identity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Religious Identity"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#opaqueOverlaidOn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates text blocks that completely obscure underlying visual content in memes"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#situationallyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates one dimension describing a situation that contradicts expectations set by another dimension in memes"@en .
